"""Per-layer mean estimator for a deep bias-free ReLU MLP.

Estimates, for each layer l of a 32-layer width-256 bias-free ReLU network, the expected post-ReLU
activation E[h_l] under standard-normal inputs x ~ N(0, I); the final layer's mean vector is the scored
quantity.

Method: Monte Carlo with variance reduction. Draw N standard-normal input samples and propagate them
through the network (z_{l+1} = h_l @ W_l, h_{l+1} = max(z_{l+1}, 0)), averaging the post-ReLU activations
per layer. Three deterministic devices reduce the sampling variance:
  1. ZCA-whitened antithetic sampling: the input block is whitened against its own sample covariance,
     with the whitening transform folded into the first-layer weights, and reflected into a
     sign/parity-symmetric quadruple, fixing the layer-1 mean and covariance exactly and cancelling
     odd-order sampling error;
  2. an intermediate-layer control variate: a closed-form Gaussian-moment propagation supplies the mean of
     an intermediate row, and the sampled deviation from it is carried forward through the realized ReLU
     gates to the final layer and subtracted, together with a small per-layer bias correction fit offline
     on synthetic networks;
  3. a per-MLP ridge-fitted energy control on two intermediate layers.
Predictions are deterministic per MLP (RNG seeded from the MLP's seed). Only the final-layer row carries a
value; earlier rows are emitted as zeros, since the challenge scores the final layer.
"""
